<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Different setups in training S-MSP.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main Results</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on five in-distribution (ID) subsets, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">single-F</span>, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">single-G</span>, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only-AND</span>, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only-OR</span>, <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only(AND-OR)</span> and their union <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">All (ID)</span>, and three aggregated out-of-distribution (OOD) suites (<span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">OOD-1/2/3 (All)</span>). The primary metric is <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">success rate</em>, defined as jointly satisfying the STL specification, remaining collision-free, and meeting kinematic feasibility. All entries in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-D RRT-Guided Local Replanning with STL Awareness ‣ V E2E Structural Moe Model ‣ Bridging Perception and Planning: Towards End-to-End Planningfor Signal Temporal Logic Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> are percentages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline (no-MoE)</span> shows limited generalization, particularly on composite formulas (notably <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only(AND-OR)</span>) and under OOD shift. A post-hoc <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TSP</span> improves feasibility and safety but yields only modest gains in STL satisfaction, since downstream filtering cannot correct upstream semantic planning errors. Relative to a simple transformer baseline, <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S-MSP</span> uses structured MoE routing aligned with STL operator families and temporal segments, reducing upstream semantic mismatches and improving reliability under shift. Quantitatively (Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-D RRT-Guided Local Replanning with STL Awareness ‣ V E2E Structural Moe Model ‣ Bridging Perception and Planning: Towards End-to-End Planningfor Signal Temporal Logic Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>), on ID it lifts “All (ID)” from <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61.50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61.50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61.50\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="71.00\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>71.00</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">71.00\%</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+9.50\,\mathrm{pp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>9.50</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+9.50\,\mathrm{pp})</annotation></semantics></math>, with pronounced gains on <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">single-G</span> <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+16.25\,\mathrm{pp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16.25</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+16.25\,\mathrm{pp})</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only-OR</span> <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+14.12\,\mathrm{pp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>14.12</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+14.12\,\mathrm{pp})</annotation></semantics></math>. With the <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TSP</span> post-processor, the advantage amplifies: “All (ID)” reaches <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.00\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>88.00</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.00\%</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.88\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>77.88</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.88\%</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+10.12\,\mathrm{pp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10.12</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+10.12\,\mathrm{pp})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">only(AND-OR)</span> improves by <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+10.17\,\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10.17</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+10.17\,\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under OOD shift, S-MSP<math id="S6.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>TSP attains the best mean across suites <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15.83\%\ \mathrm{vs.}\ 14.88\%)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>15.83</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vs</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>. 14.88</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15.83\%\ \mathrm{vs.}\ 14.88\%)</annotation></semantics></math>, led by <span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">OOD-3</span> <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+3.75\,\mathrm{pp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3.75</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>pp</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+3.75\,\mathrm{pp})</annotation></semantics></math>, and achieves best-in-column on <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5/6</annotation></semantics></math> ID subsets (plus the ID aggregate) and <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/3</annotation></semantics></math> OOD suites. Here, pp denotes percentage points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To better illustrate the behavior of our planner, we further include four full-width qualitative rollouts—one each from the ID, OOD-1, OOD-2, and OOD-3 suites, showing successful trajectories that satisfy the STL specification while remaining collision-free and kinematically feasible in Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-D RRT-Guided Local Replanning with STL Awareness ‣ V E2E Structural Moe Model ‣ Bridging Perception and Planning: Towards End-to-End Planningfor Signal Temporal Logic Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We use a factory-transport environment with four zones, white circle (<math id="S6.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>), white square (<math id="S6.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>), green circle (<math id="S6.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>), green rectangle (<math id="S6.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>), and static shelves/walls delimiting the drivable free space <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The planner is trained on the ID layout and evaluated (without finetuning) on one ID and three OOD layouts. Blue point marks the start, green is the expert trajectory, red is the planned trajectory and <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> means vehicle. Four STL tasks are showing below:</p>
<table id="S6.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S6.EGx7" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
